WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.601 --> 00:00:01.767
-Andrea,

00:00:01.767 --> 00:00:03.767
welcome to the show.
-Thanks for having me.

00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:05.100
-Congrats.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.501
"Con Pollo" is officially out.

00:00:06.501 --> 00:00:07.567
It's out this week.

00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:09.601
It's officially in bookstores.
It's out.

00:00:09.601 --> 00:00:11.601
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:13.901
Have you been
to bookstores yet to see it?

00:00:13.901 --> 00:00:16.534
-I have, but I think the most
surreal thing I've seen, so far,

00:00:16.534 --> 00:00:18.968
is that I was walking
down Midtown -- or in Midtown

00:00:18.968 --> 00:00:20.934
-Yeah.
-and I see a giant billboard

00:00:20.934 --> 00:00:22.000
with Pollo on it.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.667
And I had never
in my life imagined

00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:26.300
I would see a 30-foot Pollo
on the side of a building...

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.801
-Yeah, that you --
-...but I did.

00:00:27.801 --> 00:00:30.167
-That you drew.
-Yeah, that, too, that I drew.

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:32.534
[ Cheering and applause ]
-Yeah. You're
amazingly talented.

00:00:32.534 --> 00:00:35.234
I want to talk about your
background a little bit

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:36.934
because, a few years ago,

00:00:36.934 --> 00:00:38.734
you were doing
something very different.

00:00:38.734 --> 00:00:42.267
-Yeah, so, back in 2020,
I was in a marketing job,

00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:44.167
really questioning
what was next,

00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:45.934
what I should really be doing,

00:00:45.934 --> 00:00:48.467
and, at that point,
I decided that I knew I wanted

00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:50.467
to be doing
something more creative

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:53.567
and had my heart set on being
a full-time photographer.

00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:56.267
So, I mustered up the courage
to quit my job

00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:58.667
and leave that stability behind

00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:03.767
and decided to go freelance
in January 2020,

00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:07.033
not knowing that March 2020

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.667
-Wow.
-was coming

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:11.434
and, effectively,
because of that,

00:01:11.434 --> 00:01:14.868
I became unemployed overnight,

00:01:14.868 --> 00:01:16.567
was really like stressed
and overwhelmed.

00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:18.834
And I think we all kind of coped
in different ways

00:01:18.834 --> 00:01:20.400
-Yeah.
-in those early pandemic days.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.701
I just drew.
I drew and I drew and I drew.

00:01:22.701 --> 00:01:24.934
It brought me so much peace
and comfort.

00:01:24.934 --> 00:01:28.033
It was like this little safe
space I created for myself.

00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:29.767
And, somewhere along the line,
I was like,

00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:30.901
"You know what I miss?

00:01:30.901 --> 00:01:32.267
I miss talking to people.

00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:34.367
You know, I miss interacting
with people.
-Yeah.

00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:36.767
So, I'm going
to share these online

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:39.000
and just like have fun with it."

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.601
And, before I knew it,
I had built

00:01:40.601 --> 00:01:42.200
a little bit
of an online community

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.934
that was really supportive
of me and my artwork

00:01:44.934 --> 00:01:47.534
and started me
down this brand-new path.

00:01:47.534 --> 00:01:50.367
-What is your name, again,
on Instagram?

00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:51.033
It's @...?

00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:52.100
-It's @dreasdoodles.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.033
-@dreasdoodles.

00:01:53.033 --> 00:01:55.133
-Yeah.
-And you posted one

00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:56.601
that got a lot of attention.

00:01:56.601 --> 00:02:00.501
It was a drawing you did
of Martin Luther King Jr.

00:02:00.501 --> 00:02:01.334
-I did.

00:02:03.501 --> 00:02:06.100
[ Cheering and applause ]
-And...

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.033
[ Whistling ]

00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:09.934
...it got a lot of attention,

00:02:09.934 --> 00:02:11.868
being liked
by very famous people,

00:02:11.868 --> 00:02:14.601
including our very own Questlove

00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:16.667
liked this drawing as well.
-Yeah, he shared it.

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:18.501
Thank you.

00:02:18.501 --> 00:02:20.834
That was surreal
because this was something

00:02:20.834 --> 00:02:22.133
that really came from the heart,

00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:24.634
where I felt like it was
an important message to share,

00:02:24.634 --> 00:02:27.133
so, seeing folks like Questlove
share it,

00:02:27.133 --> 00:02:30.601
Wanda Sykes, Octavia Spencer,
I was like, "Okay,

00:02:30.601 --> 00:02:31.567
maybe there's something here.

00:02:31.567 --> 00:02:32.667
Maybe I should, you know,

00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:35.200
pursue this drawing thing
professionally.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.467
Maybe I can really use
my artistic skills

00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:40.601
to tackle different topics
and subjects.

00:02:40.601 --> 00:02:41.934
-Yeah.
And then

00:02:41.934 --> 00:02:45.033
when did you find out
that we were working together?

00:02:45.033 --> 00:02:46.534
-Actually, at a really
interesting point

00:02:46.534 --> 00:02:48.234
in my art journey.

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:50.801
I had been pitching myself
for months and months

00:02:50.801 --> 00:02:52.567
to different art directors
and editors

00:02:52.567 --> 00:02:55.200
and getting absolutely
zero responses,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.467
so, I was feeling
like really defeated.

00:02:57.467 --> 00:02:59.234
Like, "Okay, maybe I was wrong.

00:02:59.234 --> 00:03:01.267
Maybe I should go back
to the marketing job."

00:03:01.267 --> 00:03:04.767
And that's when I got an email
from Macmillan Publishing

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:06.834
and I was like, "Wait. What?"

00:03:06.834 --> 00:03:09.701
Again, in my pity party state,
like,

00:03:09.701 --> 00:03:11.367
"This publishing house
is asking me

00:03:11.367 --> 00:03:13.133
to illustrate a picture book?

00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:14.367
This can't be real.

00:03:14.367 --> 00:03:15.334
I need to google them.

00:03:15.334 --> 00:03:16.400
This is probably a scam."

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.834
Like I don't know
what's going on.

00:03:18.834 --> 00:03:19.634
I google them.

00:03:19.634 --> 00:03:20.968
They're a very real company.

00:03:20.968 --> 00:03:22.000
-[ Scoffs ] Yeah.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.734
[ Laughter ]
-And a very big deal.

00:03:23.734 --> 00:03:25.534
So, I'm like, "Oh, okay,
let me respond

00:03:25.534 --> 00:03:27.567
and kind of just see
what they say."

00:03:27.567 --> 00:03:29.634
So, I tell them, "I'm absolutely
interested in learning more

00:03:29.634 --> 00:03:32.067
about this picture book.
Tell me more."

00:03:32.067 --> 00:03:34.400
And that's when
they very calmly are like,

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.167
"Great. This picture book
is about a bilingual pollo."

00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:39.000
I'm like,
"Oh, that sounds really cool."

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.467
"Oh, by the way,
the book is written

00:03:40.467 --> 00:03:43.701
by Jimmy Fallon
and Jennifer Lopez."

00:03:43.701 --> 00:03:46.067
[ Laughter ]
¿Qué?

00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:48.200
I'm sitting in like
my little pity party,

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.701
gray on gray outfit in the rain,
just like --

00:03:50.701 --> 00:03:51.968
[ Laughter ]
-Gray on gray.

00:03:51.968 --> 00:03:53.334
-Yeah, it's my outfit.

00:03:53.334 --> 00:03:57.567
Just like so sad,
looking at this email

00:03:57.567 --> 00:03:59.501
and there's like a bunch
of different scenarios playing

00:03:59.501 --> 00:04:00.767
in my brain.
I'm like, "Okay, well,

00:04:00.767 --> 00:04:01.901
how is this all going
to play out?"

00:04:01.901 --> 00:04:03.567
And I can tell you,
wholeheartedly,

00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:05.067
none of that included this,

00:04:05.067 --> 00:04:07.100
[ Laughter ]
like this, at all.
-Right.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:08.667
-Or a 30-foot Pollo.

00:04:08.667 --> 00:04:11.567
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:04:11.567 --> 00:04:12.234
-This is it.

00:04:12.234 --> 00:04:14.300
-I mean, right?

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:15.234
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:15.234 --> 00:04:16.734
-So, once --
-I'm so happy.

00:04:16.734 --> 00:04:18.667
-Yeah, once I kind of processed
that, I'm like,

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:21.334
"Well, I'm obviously going
to say say yes."

00:04:21.334 --> 00:04:22.667
And then I make a promise
to myself.

00:04:22.667 --> 00:04:24.133
I'm like, "Okay,
you're not going to be able

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:25.434
to control the outcome of this,

00:04:25.434 --> 00:04:28.234
but what you can control is
designing the cutest,

00:04:28.234 --> 00:04:30.267
baddest chicken
the world has ever seen

00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:31.534
[ Laughter ]
-That's right.
-"and hope

00:04:31.534 --> 00:04:33.234
that everyone loves it."

00:04:33.234 --> 00:04:34.367
-And I wanted to show you this.

00:04:34.367 --> 00:04:36.968
We actually --
We made it into a plushie.

00:04:36.968 --> 00:04:38.067
[ Awws and applause ]

00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:39.501
This is Pollo.

00:04:39.501 --> 00:04:42.300
[ Cheering and applause ]
-He's so cute!

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.667
-This is you!
You drew this!

00:04:43.667 --> 00:04:44.868
-I know.
-You made this!

00:04:44.868 --> 00:04:46.567
-That's yours!

00:04:46.567 --> 00:04:48.601
She's so cute, right?

00:04:48.601 --> 00:04:50.467
-So cute!
[ Laughs ]

00:04:50.467 --> 00:04:52.300
[ Cheering and applause
continue ]

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:54.434
-I'm so thankful.
You're so talented.

00:04:54.434 --> 00:04:56.400
This is just the beginning
of many, many things.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.701
And thank you so much
for everything

00:04:57.701 --> 00:04:59.767
and coming on the show, Andrea.

00:04:59.767 --> 00:05:01.167
[ Cheering and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:02.667
-Andrea Campos, everybody.

00:05:02.667 --> 00:05:04.801
"Con Pollo" is out now!

